# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Secrets — never commit credentials
.env
backend/.env
deploy/.env
*.key

# Generated runtime artifacts
.env.ports
data/account_snapshot.json

# Engine-generated debate records. The twice-daily cycle writes one JSON + one MD per held position
# per run — roughly 30 files a day, which in two days added 60 to a repo where they are dead weight:
# the same records are persisted to the `debates` / `judgments` tables (migration 004 + the
# debate_store service), which is what calibration and every query actually read. Verified before
# untracking: all 68 on-disk records were present in the database, including every tracked one.
#
# They stay on disk — logs/debates is the engine's primary write and /api/debate/records reads it.
# This only stops them entering git.
#
# NOTE FOR ANYONE REBASING THIS COMMIT, or cloning after it: `git rm --cached` records a path
# DELETION in the commit, and git cannot tell that apart from a real delete when the commit is later
# replayed onto a tree where the files are tracked. Rebasing this very change removed all sixteen
# from the working tree on the machine that wrote them; they were restored with
# `git show <ref>:<path> > <path>`. The database copy is what makes that recoverable rather than a
# loss, which is the argument for untracking them in the first place.
#
# HAND-WRITTEN debate write-ups are NOT covered by this: they are named by date and subject
# (2026-06-03-debate-1-best-path.md) rather than by the engine's dbt-<timestamp>-<hash>, and they
# are documents someone wrote rather than output a machine produced. They stay tracked.
logs/debates/dbt-*

# Cycle reports, written twice a day by bin/scheduled_cycle.sh. Same churn as the debate records
# above, with one difference worth stating: these are NOT in the database. They are a RENDERING of
# things that are — the account snapshot, the scan survivors, and the debate decisions — so nothing
# unique is lost by keeping them out of git, but they cannot be regenerated for a past date either.
# They remain on disk on the host that produced them.
logs/reports/*-open.md
logs/reports/*-close.md
# Written by bin/stack_health.sh every 5 minutes: per-machine runtime state, not source.
data/stack_health.json
logs/refresh/
logs/cron/

# Node / Next.js (frontend)
node_modules/
frontend/.next/
frontend/node_modules/
# tsc -b incremental build state — machine-local, rewritten by every build.
*.tsbuildinfo

# Data caches
*.csv
data/cache/

# Local convenience symlink to the canonical quality bar at the projects root.
# Market data copied from the T-Drive external disk (bulk vendor exports, ~4.3 GB).
# Never committed: too large for git, and it is licensed third-party data.
# Ingested into Postgres by the loaders; the DB is the working copy, this is the archive.
data/market/

# Postgres credentials (bin/db_up.sh generates this on first run).
db/.env

# Database dumps (bin/db_backup.sh). Local safety copies only — they contain the full DB and
# would bloat git; DR-grade off-host copies are a separate concern (see the script header).
data/backups/
